/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123312 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123312 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123312 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "686cd5ae-f5fb-5c23-a23d-08b277ca72a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123313 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123313 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123313 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69d8cedf-2717-58d0-9115-0ac9a9cc3095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124330 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124330 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is invertible? \n\n- $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\n- $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=0$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d135b6c2-8257-5ba5-ac15-3d43a9abd3d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124330 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/40/407378634431fde7e66d07a36239276173d6ae121f22ef4567cb1f2b28688624.png) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124435 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124435 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ then $\\textrm{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\n- $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})\\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2086adfe-9a09-5b60-8f5e-9edb0c700b98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124435 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/36/360d6855ad322ec1ffbfdae960754f04522a33ded3e3a7e15711f0099b312ef2.png) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3,$ the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124436 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124436 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/e6/e6be87200a2cbb3709d3f398fefad0c547071edb703f88d8859d6ee0c74cc5dd.png) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "427d3dc9-9ae8-5c40-b75c-519db84f2bc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124436 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/32/32e958f4890d733ebc1856a8ca317ff6b52ed797f04a01369d6356c4130240c8.png) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124521 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272291 #edb/ref 