ef 17592186045417] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "92e4f0f3-75fd-5dde-9078-a3a161f5bd6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125118 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38033" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125118 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125118 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{8}\\to {R}^{8}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $T$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(T)<8$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2b1c7be-1664-54ba-8015-a807eb2f1187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125118 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{8}$ into ${R}^{8},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=8 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 8.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{rank}(T)<8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125164 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125164 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125164 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1,-1]^{T}\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$\n- $T$ is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a059dc0b-5296-5e25-93e6-3ffd38125f6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125164 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{2}$ into ${R}^{2},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=2 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not lie in $\\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125404 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125404 #edb/ref 1061 20018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125404 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6244a8ce-91c2-5c7a-8b6b-8f1b8c3887ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125404 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125405 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125405 #edb/ref 1061 20020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125405 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "260959de-4a00-5de6-b086-941592cccc26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125405 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125596 #edb/ref 1061 37610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125596 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd09bb01-66a3-55db-bfd3-d51ed79661a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125596 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125597 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125597 #edb/ref 1061 37611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125597 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0cb9a218-ad79-5dd3-b698-91a9f29f4e9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125597 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125784 #edb/ref 1064 "A linear transformation ![](images/93/9389ee0220ab92f8f743e705c7f9bef43f0f5c1e6122790e619ae64d99e4a480.svg) is **invertible** if there is another linear transformation ![](images/25/256c8d685bae4ddedcbfb4f972f5dcbaa65a00bf9853128bcf61a5f0fbc44052.svg) such that \n\n![](images/e4/e43ee86a504bad240bf68cc1110136b16fbb9ddb7bb250e4146582d928685821.svg)\n\nfor every vector ![](images/80/807f62e69b36dbb97ce90919d746dc86cd890eaeab263a25d6287af18af8a598.svg) The transformation ![](images/25/256c8d685bae4ddedcbfb4f972f5dcbaa65a00bf9853128bcf61a5f0fbc44052.svg) is called the **inverse** of ![](images/43/43a304a55ccd9c3f567bc9ca58aa2fb6b9aa0f8847f74a7c3dbbab3519729bb3.svg) An invertible linear transformation is also called a **bijection**. \n\nWe have the following theorem regarding invertible linear transformations: \n\n> *If ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is the standard matrix of ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg), then ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) is invertible if and only if ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is invertible*.\n\nRecall that the invertible matrix theorem in terms of dimension, rank, and nullity states that if ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is an ![](images/3e/3e214695b86d4170ea46f8cd0f8a4fd78e3d1c3a6abab1e8de1e3c35634c10a1.svg) matrix, then \n\n> ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is invertible ![](images/14/14f4479e361c0db347e9c3da0135114e8b59f7a26d3533f48a89a248501d7d00.svg)\n> ![](images/28/281f907860a395891d590680f527a1eee8be046879da8f3607669a22cae5e5d2.svg)\n\nIn the theorem above, we can make the following swaps, where ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) is a linear transformation with the standard matrix ![](images/70/70836ef8c789fb64a4b4a4b29a48074c146b8374116c1eeca4e3251def9e7d80.svg)\n![](images/96/9607abc75a2c67c4a11635cb9c84d6f575582d9961a822bdbf3d5e056536a9ce.svg)\n\nAs a result, we obtain the **invertible matrix theorem in terms of linear transformations:**\n\n> ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) is a bijection (invertible) ![](images/dc/dccddb0dd79fd52c99a28752fdcad781611226a29dc7f807411c349815f8442b.svg)\n> ![](images/14/14f4479e361c0db347e9c3da0135114e8b59f7a26d3533f48a89a248501d7d00.svg) ![](images/c1/c15eeb