[17592186272285 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expressions are ${R}^{n}$ and $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272287 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450774 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272287 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450775 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272287 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450776 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272287 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141460" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272287 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272287 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is not invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272287 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c894da3b-5f0a-59a9-8060-52795d04ecee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272287 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272291 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272291 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272291 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$ for all $k\\in R$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$\n- $T$ is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "496f9381-a057-5d31-a929-726bf731d70b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272291 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451257 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186326728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451258 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186326728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451259 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186326728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38907" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326728 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n- **II.** $T$ is invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186326728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d76ba203-8385-5ab0-a588-ad7d93b64899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326728 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ we must have that $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5,$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364132 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364132 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364132 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364132 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1888a5e7-c78d-5503-b0b1-14a906b14550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364133 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364133 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364133 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0378cd55-aff8-50e6-9d25-9722e86e2c06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364134 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364134 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364134 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47f7fcda-7cdd-5fac-b178-9b5279c586b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364135 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364135 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364135 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8f6eded-d14d-5102-a8cf-e9c780b59fc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364136 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the statements is true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364136 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364136 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c87266eb-6f2d-5861-bb16-88682d737679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364137 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364137 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364137 #