db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417702 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417702 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417702 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186417696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417702 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4b17b4e5-ae42-5c71-8493-ac975ab35b5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454696 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186417703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454697 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186417703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454698 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186417703 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417703 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417703 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186417703 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0464e7a7-ce22-55cd-90ec-4b6a71a12079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417703 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that  $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417705 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "291db6d3-4728-529e-a9be-f15ca20db25d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417705 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186417704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417706 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186417705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417706 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3efa04eb-3c5c-549f-ac5a-28d6ca38cb8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417706 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417707 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186417706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417707 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8823976c-e582-552f-a786-db586ba8c928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417707 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186417703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186449640 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186449640 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186449640 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f6e6c5d7-e923-5b79-aeca-92ce53c73074" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186449641 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186449641 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186449641 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "709274c3-c137-53e6-a5e6-794fdfe9a85a" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186449642 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186449642 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186449642 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c7181c58-f5c1-540e-9c79-9a178187acc7" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186449643 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [175